WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.891 --> 00:00:05.474
(dramatic adventure music)

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.760
- The 2010s was the decade
of the superhero movie.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.110
We saw the first three phases

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.830
of the Marvel Cinematic Universe along

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.930
with a bunch of onscreen
appearances of heroes

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.440
from DC, the X-Men, and even Pixar.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.120
Here are the top moments and
scenes from superhero movies

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.290
in the last decade.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.380
- Just leave her alone and
everything will be fine.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:27.200
- The anti superhero film
"Kick-Ass" helped usher in

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.877
the decade of the superhero.

00:00:28.877 --> 00:00:31.480
The movie is probably
remembered for this scene

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.100
where the 11 year old Hit
Girl takes out an entire room

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.163
of henchmen.

00:00:35.163 --> 00:00:37.133
- Meenie, miney, mo.

00:00:38.923 --> 00:00:42.010
- The R rated superhero flick
didn't start and stop there.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:45.090
- Oh, oh hello.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.565
- 2016's "Deadpool" was
an envelope pushing smash.

00:00:48.565 --> 00:00:51.310
- (beep) Did I leave the stove on?

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:54.130
- The movie's opening car
chase truly exemplifies

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.120
the spirit of the film
where you have Deadpool

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.710
just breaking the fourth
wall to talk to the audience

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.455
while taking out the bad guys.

00:01:00.455 --> 00:01:02.175
(Deadpool groans)

00:01:02.175 --> 00:01:04.235
- Rich Corinthian leather.

00:01:04.235 --> 00:01:05.740
- Ugh.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.390
- We'll say Ryan Reynold's
third attempt at being

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:12.570
in a superhero movie went a lot better.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.120
- Stop or we will shoot.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.070
- Freeze him Charles.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.030
- I can't.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:16.880
- [Cop] Hands up!

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.810
- The X-Men prequels
started off really strong

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.250
early in the decade and then
ended on a disastrous note.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.220
Let's just cherish this
shoot in Quicksilver time,

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.550
which is one of the
coolest slow motion shots

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:30.614
since "The Matrix's" bullet time.

00:01:30.614 --> 00:01:34.373
(adventurous music)

00:01:34.373 --> 00:01:35.206
(bad guys groan)

00:01:35.206 --> 00:01:37.870
The super fun sequel
rightly teamed up Scott Lang

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:38.970
and Hope Van Dyne.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.290
The movie's most memorable
scene comes when The Wasp

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.210
has to fend for herself in the kitchen.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:44.880
Meat tenderizers are flying at her,

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.640
she has to shrink and
grow and deal with all

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.370
these other related kitchen objects.

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:51.446
And it's really a chance for
Evangeline Lilly to shine.

00:01:51.446 --> 00:01:52.785
(knives fly)

00:01:52.785 --> 00:01:56.285
(soaring uplifting music)

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.480
Marvel waited way too
long to make it's first

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:03.720
female superhero movie.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:07.320
11 years into the MCU the
studio delivered Captain Marvel

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.480
and gave us this scene showing
everybody why Carol Danvers

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.640
is the most powerful hero in
the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.110
Carol is being held by
the Supreme Intelligence,

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.244
rips out her power dampener
and delivers this line.

00:02:19.244 --> 00:02:22.723
- I've been fighting with
one arm tied behind my back,

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.034
but what happens when,

00:02:26.034 --> 00:02:29.280
(electricity crackles)

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.483
I'm finally set free.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.530
- Then she fully powers
up into her binary form

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.705
and we see just how powerful
Captain Marvel really is.

00:02:37.705 --> 00:02:40.288
(spooky music)

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.520
For Guardians we could've
gone with Groot dancing

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.730
or even Quill dancing, but
sometimes a memorable moment,

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.210
it's not even about the characters.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.410
Take this scene where
the Guardians fly into

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.500
the lawless Nowhere and David
Bowie's "Moonage Daydream"

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.417
is blasting on the soundtrack.

00:02:57.417 --> 00:03:01.030
♪ Freak out in a moonage
daydream oh yeah ♪

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.940
Not only is it full of
awesome establishing shots

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.350
further building out Marvel's
galactic onscreen presence,

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.470
but unknown to us at the
time, it also sets up

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.323
the location of a pivotal
scene from "Infinity War".

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:20.810
Freak out,

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:21.950
far out,

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:22.783
indeed.

00:03:24.817 --> 00:03:26.795
(soaring mystical music)

00:03:26.795 --> 00:03:29.017
- [Mera] Welcome home.

00:03:29.017 --> 00:03:31.990
(soaring mystical music)

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:35.020
- Speaking of sweeping
landscapes, how about the first

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.870
glimpse of Atlantis in "Aquaman."

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.710
Who could've thought the
Atlantis of the comics

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:40.790
could be brought to life so vividly.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:43.360
Just watch the giant
tortoises and whales flying

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.420
over your head and let
your imagination run wild.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:49.110
Bonus points to this movie
for including a few things

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.500
constantly mocked about
Aquaman and making them

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:52.333
kind of awesome.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.040
- Okay now I can turn invisible.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.900
Selecting a bagel.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:58.660
Act super normal.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:03:59.690
- Spiderman?

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:01.910
- You know that's funny, I get that a lot.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:03.570
- Spiderman had a very weird decade.

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.520
He was portrayed by multiple actors

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.710
and was the subject of
a massive fight between

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.000
two rival studios.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.180
This was one of the
high points of Spiderman

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:12.360
on the big screen.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.926
In fact "Into the Spider-Verse"
even picked up the Oscar

00:04:14.926 --> 00:04:16.600
for best animated film.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.530
Wanna know why this movie got that Oscar?

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:20.046
Just take a look at this stunning scene

00:04:20.046 --> 00:04:22.370
where Peter B. Parker and Miles Morales

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:24.190
are swinging through the woods.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:25.980
Miles is finally starting to get the hang

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:26.813
of the web slinging.

00:04:26.813 --> 00:04:29.277
- Okay, thwip and release.

00:04:29.277 --> 00:04:30.827
Thwip and release.

00:04:30.827 --> 00:04:31.880
Thwip

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:32.713
- And release.

00:04:32.713 --> 00:04:33.551
- You're a natural.

00:04:33.551 --> 00:04:34.384
Thwip

00:04:34.384 --> 00:04:35.217
- Release!

00:04:35.217 --> 00:04:36.100
- It's one of the many
moments where this movie feels

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.820
like a comic book come to life.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:40.398
And speaking of animated superheros

00:04:40.398 --> 00:04:42.377
(raccoon growling)

00:04:42.377 --> 00:04:45.610
(fighting sounds)

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:47.907
Fans waited 14 years
between "The Incredibles"

00:04:47.907 --> 00:04:49.820
and "The Incredibles 2".

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:51.920
That first movie ended with a tease

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:55.740
of baby Jack Jack's powers,
emphasis on powers, plural.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:57.970
In the sequel, Jack
Jack's powers went berserk

00:04:57.970 --> 00:04:59.660
when he went head to head with a raccoon.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:01.206
He was zapping it, smacking it,

00:05:01.206 --> 00:05:03.147
even tried to set it on fire.

00:05:03.147 --> 00:05:05.000
Really makes me wish Pixar would get

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.947
into the superhero business full time.

00:05:06.947 --> 00:05:09.780
(inspiring music)

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:13.680
Marvel's on screen
battles have just gotten

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.910
better and better as time has gone on.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.350
This battle for Asgard
on the Rainbow Bridge

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.436
is set to Led Zepplin's
"Immigrant Song" making it

00:05:20.436 --> 00:05:23.540
the perfect combination
of song and fight scene.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.099
And when the song gets to
"Valhalla, I am coming"

00:05:26.099 --> 00:05:29.989
♪ Valhalla, I am coming ♪

00:05:29.989 --> 00:05:31.997
("Immigrant Song" continues)

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:35.590
Valkyrie, long associated with
Valhalla in Norse mythology,

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:38.700
is swaggering across the
bridge to enter the fight.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.010
And Thor's other allies,
Loki, Hulk, Korg, Heimdall,

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:45.790
and the rest of Asgard
are all doing their part

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.570
to take Hela and her forces down.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:50.250
With a brand new director,
"Thor Ragnarok" was

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.690
a breakthrough moment for the
character on the big screen

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.295
giving him much more
humor than he had before.

00:05:55.295 --> 00:05:57.090
- You're late.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.290
- You're missing an eye.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.180
- At the end of this movie,
Cap wakes up in what he thinks

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.450
is the 1940's only to discover

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.670
that he's been frozen for 70 years.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:09.760
He's eventually met by Nick Fury.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.720
The twist was executed well
and the movie also ended

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.520
with one of the most heartbreaking lines

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.316
in any superhero movie.

00:06:16.316 --> 00:06:21.316
- Yeah, I just, I had a date.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.527
- Steve Rodgers would finally

00:06:24.527 --> 00:06:26.605
get that date with Peggy in Endgame.

00:06:26.605 --> 00:06:27.438
And speaking of twists

00:06:27.438 --> 00:06:29.037
- Don't hurt the face.

00:06:29.037 --> 00:06:30.550
I'm an actor.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:34.160
- In "Iron Man 3", The Mandarin,
who we're led to believe

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.460
is the main villain, is
nothing more than an actor.

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:41.520
- Just a role, The
Mandarin, see it's not real.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.330
- It's a clever twist, but also

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.710
one of the MCU's most divisive moments.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:47.760
This never actually happened in the comics

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.140
and if you we're a fan of them,
you we're kind of deprived

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:52.400
of the chance to see
Iron Man face off against

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:53.690
one of his oldest foes.

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:56.640
Me, I feel like Marvel didn't
have a whole lot of options.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.540
It was the studios fix for a character

00:06:58.540 --> 00:06:59.990
who was developed around a whole lot

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:02.527
of racist tropes and stereotypes.

00:07:02.527 --> 00:07:05.007
- At least you talk.

00:07:05.007 --> 00:07:06.040
Who are you?

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:08.057
- It doesn't matter who we are.

00:07:08.057 --> 00:07:10.394
- "The Dark Night Rises"
was gonna have a tough time

00:07:10.394 --> 00:07:12.370
toping 2008's "The Dark Night",

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:15.440
but it came pretty close in
this thrilling opening scene,

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.600
which introduced us to Tom Hardy's Bane.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.240
It's an unforgettable opening,

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:21.560
not just because they
filmed it in real planes

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.440
and also not because all of your friends

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:26.650
had terrible Bane impressions
after this movie came out.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:30.750
- No one cared who I was
til I put on the mask.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.556
- The script says do a Bane voice but

00:07:32.556 --> 00:07:33.906
(muffled) mines really bad.

00:07:34.917 --> 00:07:36.098
(laughs)

00:07:36.098 --> 00:07:37.908
(muffled) I was born in the dark.

00:07:37.908 --> 00:07:41.107
(laughs) I can't do one.

00:07:41.107 --> 00:07:43.774
(intense music)

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:47.790
Wonder Woman makes her grand entrance,

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:51.110
a bright spot in an other
wise awful movie and a sign,

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:53.270
not fully realized at
the time, that there was

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:56.470
a brighter future ahead for
the DC Extended Universe.

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:58.839
Just try to get that theme
music out of your head.

00:07:58.839 --> 00:08:02.006
(Intense theme music)

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:07.770
We're gonna come back to
Wonder Woman in a little bit.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:11.343
- Before we get started,
does anyone want to get out?

00:08:13.936 --> 00:08:16.186
(fighting)

00:08:17.711 --> 00:08:20.070
(alarm)

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:22.310
- Now, this is among the
absolute best fight scenes

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:24.250
in the entire Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.180
and it's not on a giant battlefield.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:29.200
It's just Captain America,
a bunch of Hydra soldiers,

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.270
and a very cramped elevator.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:32.730
This scene was so memorable,

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.022
that it showed up again in "Endgame".

00:08:35.022 --> 00:08:37.439
(rock music)

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:44.460
This was by far the best of
the solo Wolverine movies.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.340
It was the dark and emotional portrayal

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.010
that the character always deserved.

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:50.080
I could choose any action
sequence from this movie

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:51.160
that gave it it's R-rating,

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:53.750
but Logan's death, really
felt like everything

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:57.689
was coming full circle, and it
was truly the end of an era.

00:08:57.689 --> 00:09:00.180
- Don't be what they made you.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.500
- Logan's entire life has been a battle

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.220
and he can finally be at peace.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:06.718
Now give us an X-23 movie already.

00:09:06.718 --> 00:09:09.070
- Where is this one from?

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:11.170
- The Bobo Ashanti tribe

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:13.327
from present-day Ghana, 19th century.

00:09:13.327 --> 00:09:15.300
- "Black Panther" was
a groundbreaking moment

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:16.207
in pop culture.

00:09:16.207 --> 00:09:19.350
It's Best Picture nomination
can attest to that,

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:22.450
but it's not the movies
hero I want to talk about.

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:25.354
It's the villain,
Killmonger, and his intro.

00:09:25.354 --> 00:09:28.000
- How do you think your
ancestors got these?

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.760
You think they paid a fare price?

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:31.760
Or did they take it like
they took everything else?

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:33.057
- Perhaps you're already thinking

00:09:33.057 --> 00:09:35.970
"Hey is it even fair to
call this guy the villain?"

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:38.163
- But I see through you.

00:09:38.163 --> 00:09:40.496
(whooshing)

00:09:42.997 --> 00:09:45.240
- "Doctor Strange" expanded the MCU

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.670
far beyond normal fighting.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.530
Nowhere is that more apparent than when

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:50.710
Doctor Strange first meets The Ancient One

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:52.863
and she takes him on quite the trip.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:55.840
- [The Ancient One] What
mysteries lie beyond

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:57.460
the reach of your senses.

00:09:57.460 --> 00:09:59.030
- The visuals feel like a callback to

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:01.620
the original Steve Ditko
creations from the 60's

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.305
and it set up a lot of what was to come

00:10:03.305 --> 00:10:07.283
with reality bending and time
travel later on in the MCU.

00:10:10.041 --> 00:10:12.291
(grunting)

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:17.780
- Mysterio takes Spiderman
through a mind bending trip

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:20.410
through a whole bunch of projections.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:22.305
It's mesmerizing and terrifying.

00:10:22.305 --> 00:10:24.713
Just look at zombie Tony Stark.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:28.070
This already feels like a sequence fans

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:31.000
will be talking about for a very long time

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.430
and other Spiderman movies,
both animated and live action,

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:36.503
are gonna have a hard time topping it.

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:41.360
The third Captain America
movie isn't just about Cap.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:44.090
During a pivotal battle
at an airport in Berlin,

00:10:44.090 --> 00:10:46.010
the Avengers found themselves divided.

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:49.040
On one side, we had Iron
Man joined by Spiderman,

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.320
Black Panther, and War Machine.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:53.380
And on the other, we had Captain America,

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:56.300
with Falcon, Winter
Soldier, and Giant Man.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.790
Scarlet Witch made cars
fly through the air,

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:01.160
Ant Man grew to the size of a skyscraper,

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:03.600
and Spiderman made "Star Wars" jokes.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:04.433
- Hey guys!

00:11:04.433 --> 00:11:07.780
You ever see that really old
movie, "Empire Strikes Back"?

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:08.613
- Jesus, Tony.

00:11:08.613 --> 00:11:09.446
How old is this guy?

00:11:09.446 --> 00:11:10.740
- I don't know, I didn't carbon-date him.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:12.450
- This made watching the team fall apart

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:13.905
completely worth it.

00:11:13.905 --> 00:11:17.655
(dramatically intense music)

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:24.560
DC got ahead of the MCU with
this female led superhero film.

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:26.410
Set during the first World War,

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:29.510
includes this thrilling
scene set in No Man's Land

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:31.750
where Diana marches across the battlefield

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:35.360
drawing all the enemy fire
so the soldiers can advance.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:37.680
If this sequence doesn't
make you a Wonder Woman fan,

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:38.770
nothing could.

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:41.060
So, we've hit the top 3 and, yes,

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:44.089
it's just going to be the
Avengers movies here on out.

00:11:44.089 --> 00:11:44.922
When you think of superhero films

00:11:44.922 --> 00:11:46.340
from the last decade or so,

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:48.718
this is probably the first
image that comes to mind.

00:11:48.718 --> 00:11:51.558
(Hulk yelling)

00:11:51.558 --> 00:11:54.975
(Avengers theme playing)

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.100
At that point, we'd
been waiting four years

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:00.880
since the first Iron Man movie to see

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:03.590
the core Avengers all
together in one shot,

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:05.040
suited up and ready for action.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.593
And this image is just straight up iconic.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:17.500
It's rare to see a superhero
movie end on such a downer,

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:20.050
but the snap in "Infinity
War" proved to be

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:22.780
the "Empire Strikes
Back" of Avengers movies.

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:25.120
And we we're left to
wonder for an entire year

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:27.050
how those heroes would come back.

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:28.610
Because the movies make way too much money

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:30.352
to not bring them back.

00:12:30.352 --> 00:12:33.602
(Avengers theme plays)

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:38.174
This was the hardest movie
to pick a scene from,

00:12:38.174 --> 00:12:40.790
so we're gonna cheat and
say the entire third act

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:44.120
because technically it's the
ending to 21 other movies

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:45.710
and it delivered.

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:47.270
You have Thor, Cap, and Iron Man

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:49.140
coming together for one last stand.

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:51.090
A throwback to the first Avengers movie

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:53.344
when they first came together
and fought over Loki.

00:12:53.344 --> 00:12:55.670
And if you saw this in theaters,

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:58.080
you really thought one of
them was gonna die here.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:01.080
But no, the Russo's
decided to instead deliver

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:03.880
on more than a decade
of teases and build ups.

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:05.920
Cap shows he's worthy by wielding Mjolnir

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:07.180
and summoning the lightning.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:09.260
He straps on his shield for what he thinks

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:12.150
will be the last time and
Sam gives us a callback

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:15.540
to Winter Soldier to let Cap
know the cavalry has arrived.

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:17.210
- [Sam] On your left.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:19.124
- Don't say it, don't you say it.

00:13:19.124 --> 00:13:19.957
- On your left.

00:13:19.957 --> 00:13:20.790
- Come on!

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:22.340
- It looks like all of Wakanda is there.

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:23.740
All the wizards are there.

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:25.760
Valkyrie has shown up on her pegasus.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:28.360
Even Pepper Potts is
there in the Rescue Suit.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:31.230
And Captain America delivers
the iconic battle cry

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:33.393
that fans have been waiting years to hear.

00:13:34.271 --> 00:13:35.762
- Avengers!

00:13:41.055 --> 00:13:41.896
Assemble.

00:13:41.896 --> 00:13:42.970
- They could have just left it at that.

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:45.930
But nope the battle still
gave us some great moments.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:47.850
Tony and Pepper fight back to back.

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:49.580
Tony finally hugs Peter.

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:51.130
There's the gauntlet relay.

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:53.020
All the female Avengers come together.

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:56.160
If you saw this in theaters,
especially opening weekend,

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.400
then you know how much
the audience cheered

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:00.180
for each and every one of those moments.

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:03.200
And how dusty the theater
got for Tony's funeral.

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:05.080
That ending is why I
paid to see this movie

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:06.680
in theaters three times.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.870
- [Producer] You saw it
three times in theaters?

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:09.750
- You didn't?

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:11.330
Did we miss any of your favorites?

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:13.023
Let us know in the comments.

00:14:14.482 --> 00:14:17.240
- [Captain America] That is America's ass.

